<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>





<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 9 Practice</title>

    <p workspace=".2in">
        <em>Identify situations in which it is appropriate to
                            use induction and state the procedure for proving a
                            statement by induction.</em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        How to identify when you can prove a statement by induction. You can do 
                        induction when there is a starting place, and an <q>up by one every time</q> or <q>discrete step</q>
                        aspect to the theorem. E.g., if the theorem starts <q> for all <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m> </q>
                            or <q>for all <m>n\in\mathbb{Z}</m> with <m>n\ge -2</m></q>. You cannot use induction for all
                             <m>n\in\mathbb{R}</m> (no discrete step) nor for all <m>n\in \mathbb{Z}</m> (no starting point).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                      What the two steps for induction are (base case and inductive hypothesis).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        How to write out the two steps are, specific to a theorem statement.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        You will NOT need to complete an entire proof by induction.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice From Class</title>

        <p>
            Anything in the Chapter 4 worksheets. Weekly Practice 7. See also the extra help for induction under the 
            getting help folder on Blackboard. 
        </p>

    </paragraphs>


    
<exercise>
            <introduction>
               <p> Choose the statements that are appropriate to prove by induction and explain
                why.
                For each statement you choose, state what your steps would be in a proof by
                induction.
                (Your response should be specific to the statement.) </p>
            </introduction>
    
                    <task>
                        <statement>
                        
                        <p>
                            For all <m>a\in\mathbb{Z}</m> the equation
                            <m>ax^{3} + ax+a = 0</m> does not have a solution that is a
                            natural number.
                    
                        </p>
            
                        </statement>
                        <solution>
                            <p>
                                This is not a proof by induction since you are proving a statement for all <m>a\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
                                (no starting point).
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For each natural number <m>n</m>, <m>3\mid 4^{n} - 1</m>.
                        </p>
                         </statement>
                         <solution>
                            <p>
                                This can be proven by induction. Let <m>S_n</m> be the statement <m>3\mid 4^{n}-1</m>. For 
                                the base case, we show <m>S_1</m> or that <m>3\mid 4^{1} - 1</m>. For the inductive step, 
                                let <m>k\in\mathbb{N}</m> and assume <m>S_k</m> is true which is that <m>3\mid 4^{k}-1</m>. Then
                                we prove <m>S_{k+1}</m> which is that <m>3\mid 4^{k+1}-1</m>.
                            </p>
                         </solution>
                    </task>
                       

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For all integers <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, if <m>x</m> is even and
                            <m>y</m> is odd, <m>x+y</m> is odd.
                        </p>
                        
                                                </statement>
                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                       This statement is not proved by induction since it's for all 
                                                       integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. The integers don't have a 
                                                       starting point to be used as a base case.
                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For all natural numbers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, if <m>mn</m> is
                            an even integer, then <m>m</m> is even or <m>n</m> is even.
                        </p>
                                                </statement>

                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                        This statement is not proved by induction. Even though it says 
                                                        for all natural numbers <m>m</m> and <m>n</m>, we haven't done
                                                        an induction proof that has<m>2</m> variables like this.
                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>

                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For all natural numbers <m>n</m> with <m>n\ge 4</m>,
                            <m>n! \gt;2^{n}</m>.
                        </p>
                                                </statement>
                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                        For this one we do use inductions, since it starts <q>for all natural 
                                                            numbers <m>n</m> with <m>n\geq 4</m></q> and we can see how 
                                                            we might move from one case to the next. We would define <m>S_n</m> for 
                                                            all <m>n\geq 4</m> as <m>n! gt; 2^n</m>. For the base case, prove <m>S_4</m>
                                                            or that <m>4! \gt 2^4 </m>. For the inductive step, let <m>k\in \mathbb{N}</m>
                                                            and assume <m>S_k</m> is true which is that <m>k!\gt 2^k </m>. We then prove that
                                                            <m>S_{k+1}</m> is true, which is that <m>(k+1)! \gt 2^{k+1}</m>.
                                                       
                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>

                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>a,b\in\mathbb{Z}</m> and <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>.
                            For all <m>m\in \mathbb{N}</m>, if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{n}</m>
                            then <m>a^{m} \equiv b^{m} \pmod{n}</m>.
                        </p>

        
                                                </statement>
                                                <solution>
                                                    <p>
                                                        We can prove this statement by induction (though we haven't yet done
                                                        one like it in class). We induction on <m>n</m>. For each <m>n\in\mathbb{N}</m>
                                                        let <m>S_n</m> be if <m> a \equiv b\pmod{n}</m> then <m>a^m \equiv b^m \pmod{n}</m>. 
                                                        We prove the base case by proving <m>S_1</m> which is <q>if <m>a \equiv b \pmod{n}</m></q>
                                                        then <m>a^1 \equiv b^1 \pmod{n} </m>. Now, for the inductive step let <m>k\in\mathbb{N}</m>. 
                                                        Assume <m>S_k</m> or that if <m>a\equiv b \pmod{n}</m> then <m>a^k b\pmod{n}</m>.
                                                        We would show <m>S_{k+1}</m> or that if <m>a\equiv b\pmod{n}</m> then <m>a^{k+1}\equiv b \pmod{n}</m>.
                                                    </p>
                                                </solution>
                    </task>

                    <task>
                          <statement>
                        <p>
                            For all real numbers <m>x</m>, if <m>x^{2} = 2</m>, then
                            <m>x</m> is irrational. </p>
                             </statement>

                             <solution>
                                <p>
                                    We would not use induction here since the statement is for all real numbers <m>x</m>. The real numbers
                                    have neither a starting point or a <q>discrete step</q> where the next step would be the same amount more.
                                    (I.e., there's no one real number that you can say is <q>next</q> after  <m>3</m>).
                                </p>
                             </solution>
                        </task>
                                               

                   
                    </exercise>


        </handout>